 those behind  the attack and those that ordered it  now this has come after we first  initially  heard that this was an incident it was  being investigated we thought it was an  accident  but suspicious nevertheless some  lawmakers here said that there was a  possibility that it could have been  sabotaged  or infiltration now this is the head of  iran's atomic organization  clearly now saying that this is more  than just an accident this is  uh an attack and it comes just a day  after iran  uh began gave the news that it was using  advanced centrifuges to enrich uranium  and this is coming as the u.s is about  to resume  nuclear talks with tehran on the 2015  nuclear deal  how might it affect the discussions and  negotiations  well it's definitely going to complicate  matters if iran feels that it is  being under attack by by israel  because that's where the finger is being  pointed although lawmakers and aliya  prasadi  haven't pointed directly at israel but  earlier this week on tuesday an iranian  ship  was attacked in the red sea and from  reports  israel is said to be behind that now  iran's nuclear facility has been  attacked before there's an act of  sabotage according to the iranians  last year in the summer but again iran  wants to go back  and wants these sanctions lifted and  aliyah salihe is saying  that this act is carried out by those  that oppose iran's negotiations and  oppose those sanctions  being lifted so it will definitely  complicate those talks  uh and make them even more difficult  than they already are  thank you very much for that assad big  live forest there in tehran let's get  the view now from jerusalem and speak to  harry fossett  harry as assad said there iran seems to  be pointing the finger of blame at  israel and i know this iranian nuclear  issue is featuring high on the agenda of  discussions  between israeli leaders and the visiting  u.s defense secretary but they're not on  the same page when it comes to  the iranian nuclear issue  very much not and this is the key  difference between what  uh was there under the trump  administration and the situation  now uh that president biden has been  sworn in and  not just not just on the same page  between the u.s and israel it's not  clear that  all of the israeli government is on the  same page given what we heard  at the top of the show from the defense  minister benny gans now of course he's  in this very  uneasy coalition with the prime minister  benjamin netanyahu the bitter political  rivals have just fought  an election campaign against each other  and so  while gans is talking about working with  the us to ensure that any deal  meets israel's needs and expectations  benjamin netanyahu has been talking very  vociferously as he long has for many  years against any idea  of such a deal israel has  reportedly put some very strict criteria  behind what it would accept but they're  so strict from the israeli perspective  that  it's very unlikely that any of them or  at least not all of them could be met  in any deal going forward and so what  he's been saying at the same time as  these talks have been going on  in vienna last week is that any  agreement  that would pave the way for iran to have  a nuclear weapon  in the future is one that israel would  not be bound by  and so obviously that's talking about  freedom of action in the context  that we've just been hearing now the the  meeting that we've had today  between gantz and the lloyd austin the  u.s secretary of state  has been one which they've been talking  fairly uh  positively about the long-standing  us alliance talking about plans for  future military acquisitions in the  future and so this is what lloyd austin  had to say our commitment to israel  is enduring and it is ironclad  and i pledge to continue close  consultations in order to ensure  israel's qualitative military edge and  to strengthen  israel's security and harry as  assad in tehran alluded to this is  coming at a time 